A timer that drives a reactive `if` re-renders into that slot's own anchor.

Two reactive `if`s over one var, ticked by a timer: the carousel. Each `if`
becomes a `__renderSlotN` the reactivity pass re-fires wherever the var is
written, and the parent it renders into has to be the slot's own
`display:contents` anchor -- the same node the init call uses. Threaded with the
enclosing parentRef instead, the re-fire appends in the wrong place and the
teardown removeChilds from a node that never held the child.

The re-fire lands inside the callback html hands `setInterval`, so the pass that
retargets these calls has to reach a lambda held by an assignment. It reached a
CallStmt and a CallStmt's lambda arguments, which covered the timer handler it
was written for and not `handle = setInterval(func() { ... })`.

deny out/none/html/index.html `__renderSlot0\(__n` -- the re-fire targets the slot's anchor, not the parent it was threaded with
deny out/none/html/index.html `__renderSlot1\(__n` -- likewise for the second branch

-- app.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"
import . "sngl:time"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

component Car() node {
    var a = 0

    timer(interval=1000ms, @tick { a = (a + 1) % 2 })
    stack {
        if a == 0 {
            text(value="ZERO")
        }
        if a == 1 {
            text(value="ONE")
        }
    }
}

window(title="H") {
    Car()
}
